# Agent run logs / transcripts
runs/
*.log

# Generated content bundles (`bosc export` → data/site/bundles/<slug>/, per network site).
# The contract is committed under data/site/bundle/ — README.md, schemas/,
# manifest.example.json — but the per-site feed data and the timestamped manifest are
# regenerable (and the documents feed is LFS-state-dependent), so they stay out of git.
# Regenerate any time with `bosc --site <slug> export`.
/data/site/bundles/
# Legacy single-site output path (pre-#727); ignored so a stale local bundle never lands.
/data/site/bundle/feeds/
/data/site/bundle/manifest.json

# yidam corpus mirror + reports (`watermark corpus-mirror`, and every `watermark export`;
# epic #1560/#1561/#1562). The committed corpus (entities, relationships, concepts, people,
# leads, hypotheses, [open] claims) projected into yidam node format under .yidam/corpus/.
# Regenerable, per-active-site, never a source of truth — so the tree stays out of git (like
# the content bundle). Regenerate: `watermark --site <slug> export` (or `watermark
# corpus-mirror`). The upstream pin (.yidam.toml) IS committed.
#
# The reports over that mirror are no longer written here at all: they come from the real
# `yidam` binary now (`mise run yidam-reports`), not from a retired Python replica.
# NOTE the trailing `/*` rather than `/`: git cannot re-include a file whose PARENT DIRECTORY
# is excluded, so `/.yidam/` would make the negation below silently dead.
/.yidam/*

# ...with ONE exception. The lint baseline is a REVIEWED artifact, not a regenerable one: it
# enumerates the inherited violations this repo has accepted, so `yidam lint` can gate on
# whether a change made things *worse* rather than on whether the corpus is perfect. CI needs
# it to reproduce the same verdict, and an uncommitted baseline means no ratchet at all —
# every inherited finding reads as a regression and the gate gets switched off. Re-bless
# deliberately (`yidam lint --bless`) and review the diff, exactly like an extraction.
!/.yidam/lint-baseline.yml

# ...and the authorship manifest, for the same reason: it is a REVIEWED statement about which
# files this repository did not write (a frozen import, the generated mirror), and the checks
# that read prose consult it. CI must see the same declaration a developer does, or it reports
# findings against material nobody here can act on. `yidam schema` writes its JSON Schema to
# the (ignored) .yidam/schemas/.
!/.yidam/authorship.yml

# ...and the vault configuration, which is a THIRD reviewed artifact under this otherwise
# regenerable tree (#2144, epic #2141). It declares where the corpus keeps the bytes that no longer
# live in git, and it is load-bearing for a gate: `catalog-artifact-unroutable` is ERROR-severity and
# compares every projected artifact's `vault:` against the stores declared here. An UNCOMMITTED
# config therefore does not degrade quietly — CI declares no vault and all 3,662 artifacts read as
# routed nowhere. That loudness is deliberate (see docs/artifact-vault.md), but it only works if the
# file travels with the repository.
#
# It carries no credential and must never carry one: those come from the environment
# (YIDAM_VAULT_DEFAULT_ACCESS_KEY_ID / _SECRET_ACCESS_KEY), which is why the file is safe to commit.
!/.yidam/config.toml

# RSEI v2.3.12 offline test fixture (a small committed Allen County slice zip). The
# user's global gitignore excludes *.zip, so re-include just this fixture (#1148).
!tests/fixtures/rsei/*.zip
